= Chemical equilibrium =

== Overview ==
'''Chemical equilibrium''' is a state of a reacting system in which its macroscopic composition remains constant over time under the specified conditions, even though forward and reverse molecular processes continue. For a simple reversible reaction at equilibrium, the forward and reverse reaction rates are equal. Equilibrium is dynamic, not the cessation of all reaction.

== Equilibrium and reaction quotient ==
For a reaction with reactants A and B forming C and D, the '''reaction quotient''' Q is constructed from the relative activities of the participating species and the balanced reaction's stoichiometric coefficients. At equilibrium, the quotient equals the equilibrium constant K for the specified reaction and temperature.

A large K can indicate that products are favored at equilibrium, but it says nothing by itself about how fast equilibrium is reached. Rates depend on kinetics; equilibrium composition is a thermodynamic property.

== Changing conditions ==
Changing concentrations, pressure or temperature can disturb an equilibrium. The system may react in the direction that re-establishes equilibrium under the new conditions. Le Châtelier's principle is a qualitative guide, not a substitute for quantitative mass-balance, kinetic and thermodynamic calculations.

A catalyst can increase the rates of both the forward and reverse processes and help equilibrium be reached sooner. It does not alter K or the equilibrium composition at the same temperature.

== Relation to pH and acid–base chemistry ==
[[Related to::pH|pH]] describes hydrogen-ion activity. Many acid–base reactions are equilibria, so changing solution composition, temperature or ionic environment may change pH through the speciation of acids and bases. A buffer has finite capacity and its behavior depends on the relevant equilibria; a solution's pH does not establish every other equilibrium state in that system.

== Examples and scope ==
An aqueous weak acid can coexist with its conjugate base and undissociated molecules. The proportions depend on the equilibrium constant and solution conditions. In a sealed vessel, a reversible gas reaction can reach constant partial pressures while molecular reactions continue in both directions.

== Limits and evidence ==
Simple concentration quotients approximate more rigorous activity-based expressions, particularly in nonideal mixtures. Some systems approach equilibrium very slowly; observing a constant concentration over a short interval is not by itself proof of thermodynamic equilibrium.

